The `hasStreamingAnalysis` field in `use-transcript-fetch.ts` is hardcoded to `false`, which appears suspicious but is intentional due to the hook architecture.

## Changes

- Added inline comment explaining why `hasStreamingAnalysis` is initialized to `false`
- Documented that the orchestrator layer (`use-video-processing.ts`) mutates this field to `true` when runs data indicates a streaming workflow exists

## Context

`use-transcript-fetch.ts` calls `/api/video/[videoId]` which doesn't return runs data. The `hasStreamingAnalysis` determination requires `/api/video/[videoId]/analyze` data, accessible only at the orchestrator level:

```typescript
// use-transcript-fetch.ts
const hasStreamingAnalysis = false; // No access to runs data here
return { status, hasStreamingAnalysis };

// use-video-processing.ts (orchestrator)
const result = await transcriptFetch.checkVideoStatus();
if (runsResult.streamingRun?.workflowRunId) {
  result.hasStreamingAnalysis = true; // Enhanced with runs data
}
```

This follows the established pattern where lower-level hooks return base data and the orchestrator enriches it with cross-cutting concerns.

